WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.090
GPUs are acceleration hardware for local AI, inference servers, vision models, embeddings, development workflows, and data-heavy experimentation.

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:19.345
A capable GPU can make model loading, prompt processing, image generation, vector search support, and batch inference feel practical.

00:00:19.345 --> 00:00:26.930
But the GPU only helps when the operating system, driver stack, runtime, and application can actually use it.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:34.027
Drivers sit between the Linux kernel, hardware, user-space libraries, containers, and AI applications.

00:00:34.027 --> 00:00:38.829
The kernel needs modules and firmware to communicate with the device.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:43.074
User-space libraries expose compute capabilities to runtimes.

00:00:43.074 --> 00:00:47.179
Containers need access to the host driver and device nodes.

00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:53.790
Python libraries, inference servers, and notebooks depend on that whole chain being compatible.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:59.774
There is a major difference between a detected GPU and a usable AI acceleration stack.

00:00:59.774 --> 00:01:12.091
A system may list a PCI device, but still fail to load the right driver, expose the wrong libraries, block access through permissions, or run the workload on the CPU by mistake.

00:01:12.091 --> 00:01:19.119
Good validation proves that the AI runtime can use acceleration, not merely that the hardware exists.

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:25.103
Driver stability affects performance, reliability, troubleshooting, and repeatability.

00:01:25.103 --> 00:01:28.235
An unmanaged update can break kernel modules.

00:01:28.235 --> 00:01:32.201
A random library mix can create confusing runtime errors.

00:01:32.201 --> 00:01:36.585
A system that worked yesterday may fail after a kernel upgrade.

00:01:36.585 --> 00:01:45.631
Treat GPU configuration as part of the AI system architecture, with planned versions, documentation, checks, and rollback options.
